WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.070
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.617
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit eight: Judicial System. Today you will write formal conditions by inverting had, should and were.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.807
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.278
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.373
Supreme court.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:29.254
Supreme court means the highest court in a country.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.674
The supreme court has the final word on the law.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.667
Your turn. Say: Supreme court.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.894
Supreme court.

11
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.883
Due process.

12
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:47.624
Due process means fair legal procedures that the state must follow.

13
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:51.412
Due process protects people from unfair punishment.

14
00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:54.936
Your turn. Say: Due process.

15
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.824
Due process.

16
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:00.851
Legal precedent.

17
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.730
Legal precedent means an earlier court decision that guides later ones.

18
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:10.162
The judge followed a legal precedent from 1990.

19
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.751
Your turn. Say: Legal precedent.

20
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.007
Legal precedent.

21
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.030
Trial by jury.

22
00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:25.861
Trial by jury means a trial in which citizens decide whether the accused is guilty.

23
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.947
Trial by jury gives ordinary people a role in justice.

24
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:33.237
Your turn. Say: Trial by jury.

25
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.635
Trial by jury.

26
00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:39.605
Judicial review.

27
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:44.369
Judicial review means the power of courts to check whether laws are lawful.

28
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:47.610
Judicial review struck down the unfair law.

29
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:50.823
Your turn. Say: Judicial review.

30
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.167
Judicial review.

31
00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:56.906
Appeal.

32
00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:01.402
Appeal means a request to a higher court to change a decision.

33
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:04.469
The lawyer filed an appeal the next morning.

34
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.596
Your turn. Say: Appeal.

35
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:11.411
Appeal.

36
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.244
Rule of law.

37
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.606
Rule of law means the principle that everyone must obey the same laws.

38
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:21.530
The rule of law applies to presidents too.

39
00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:24.978
Your turn. Say: Rule of law.

40
00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:29.554
Rule of law.

41
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:31.329
Sentencing.

42
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.744
Sentencing means the stage when a court decides the punishment.

43
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.371
Sentencing guidelines aim to ensure consistency.

44
00:02:39.983 --> 00:02:42.299
Your turn. Say: Sentencing.

45
00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:46.285
Sentencing.

46
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.774
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.617 --> 00:02:57.055
Visal, a judge writes 'Had the police followed due process, the case would not have collapsed.' Where is 'if'?

48
00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:09.362
It is omitted. In formal writing, we can invert had, should or were instead of using if: Had they followed = If they had followed. The meaning is identical.

49
00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.066
What about the other forms?

50
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:24.733
Should the appeal fail, the sentence will stand. Were the supreme court to change the precedent, many cases would be reopened. Each is more formal than a normal if-clause.

51
00:03:25.817 --> 00:03:27.282
Does the negative change?

52
00:03:28.383 --> 00:03:39.787
Yes: Had the jury not heard the evidence... or Were it not for judicial review, ... Note that we do not contract: we say 'Had they not', not 'Hadn't they'.

53
00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:42.608
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.679
Grammar focus: Conditional inversion: Had and Should and Were.

55
00:03:47.979 --> 00:03:49.235
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.343
Had the police followed due process, the case would not have collapsed.

57
00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.656
Should the appeal fail, the sentence will stand.

58
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.372
Were the court to ignore legal precedent, many cases would be reopened.

59
00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:12.741
Drop 'if' and invert: Had + subject + past participle (third), Should + subject + base (possible), Were + subject + to-verb (unreal). Formal use only.

60
00:04:13.483 --> 00:04:17.181
Be careful! Don't say: If had they known, it would end.

61
00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.470
Say: Had they known, it would have ended.

62
00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:23.479
Drop 'if' when you invert 'had'.

63
00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:27.058
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.471
Fairness Before the Law.

65
00:04:29.771 --> 00:04:32.886
A fair court protects both the accused and the community.

66
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.780
Had due process been ignored, a verdict could be overturned on appeal.

67
00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:44.226
Should a decision seem unfair, the supreme court may order judicial review of the law itself.

68
00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:49.868
Were judges free to disregard legal precedent, the rule of law would soon weaken.

69
00:04:50.867 --> 00:04:55.757
A trial by jury and a transparent sentencing process also help citizens trust the courts.

70
00:04:56.867 --> 00:05:00.892
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.992
blank the police followed due process, the case would have stood.

72
00:05:05.292 --> 00:05:08.458
A, Had. B, If had. C, Have.

73
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.524
The answer is A: Had.

74
00:05:14.683 --> 00:05:17.972
A request to a higher court to change a decision is an blank.

75
00:05:18.272 --> 00:05:21.461
A, precedent. B, appeal. C, jury.

76
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.802
The answer is B: appeal.

77
00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.402
Should the appeal fail, the sentence blank.

78
00:05:30.702 --> 00:05:34.344
A, would stand. B, will stand. C, stood.

79
00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.027
The answer is B: will stand.

80
00:05:41.183 --> 00:05:43.067
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:50.921
A, Were it not for the jury, he would lose.. B, Was it not for the jury, he would lose.. C, Be it not for the jury, he would lose..

82
00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:57.836
The answer is A: Were it not for the jury, he would lose..

83
00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:03.003
According to the reading, a fair court protects the accused and the blank.

84
00:06:03.303 --> 00:06:07.026
A, press. B, community. C, police.

85
00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:12.682
The answer is B: community.

86
00:06:13.867 --> 00:06:17.181
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.017 --> 00:06:21.607
Why is 'due process' considered essential to a fair judicial system?

88
00:06:30.417 --> 00:06:31.917
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.217 --> 00:06:56.190
Due process means that the state must follow fair, known procedures before it punishes anyone. It guarantees the right to be informed of charges, to defend oneself and to have the evidence tested in court. Without it, the powerful could imprison opponents without proof. Due process therefore protects the innocent and makes verdicts more trustworthy.

90
00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.188
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.083 --> 00:07:04.836
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and conditional inversion: had and should and were.

92
00:07:05.136 --> 00:07:09.146
Today's value is justice. Stand up for what is fair for everyone.

93
00:07:09.446 --> 00:07:11.948
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:12.248 --> 00:07:15.422
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

